1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,826
This network reads product reviews
one letter at a time.

2
00:00:04,826 --> 00:00:09,336
It was trained to do one thing,
and one thing only: guess the next letter.

3
00:00:09,636 --> 00:00:11,258
Now watch one of its neurons

4
00:00:11,258 --> 00:00:13,527
while it reads this customer review.

5
00:00:13,627 --> 00:00:15,328
It climbs as the praise piles up,

6
00:00:15,328 --> 00:00:18,136
and drops the moment the complaint begins.

7
00:00:18,386 --> 00:00:22,277
Nobody told it what happy means.
It never saw a star rating.

8
00:00:22,577 --> 00:00:25,358
So why would a letter-guesser
grow a mood detector?

9
00:00:28,458 --> 00:00:30,983
What does the mood have to do
with the next letter?

10
00:00:31,645 --> 00:00:34,051
First, the game.
The network reads a review,

11
00:00:34,051 --> 00:00:37,242
and before each letter,
it bets on what comes next.

12
00:00:37,342 --> 00:00:38,931
Its first bets are spread out.

13
00:00:38,931 --> 00:00:40,933
A few letters into the word “great”,

14
00:00:40,933 --> 00:00:42,171
it's already sure.

15
00:00:42,321 --> 00:00:46,232
Then it sees the real letter,
gets nudged toward it, and moves on.

16
00:00:46,232 --> 00:00:48,644
Each review is its own answer key.

17
00:00:48,794 --> 00:00:50,031
That's the whole job.

18
00:00:50,031 --> 00:00:53,991
No stars, no labels, nothing about mood.

19
00:00:54,091 --> 00:00:57,778
In 2017, researchers at OpenAI played it

20
00:00:57,778 --> 00:01:00,493
over 82 million Amazon reviews:

21
00:01:00,493 --> 00:01:03,294
a month of guessing, one letter at a time.

22
00:01:03,794 --> 00:01:06,277
Your turn: how does this review go on?

23
00:01:08,577 --> 00:01:11,269
Not strong enough.
That's what the network writes.

24
00:01:11,269 --> 00:01:15,498
Change only the first sentence to:
“Best thing I bought this year,”

25
00:01:15,498 --> 00:01:18,787
and the same words go on: “a great price.”

26
00:01:18,937 --> 00:01:21,385
The words right before the blank
were the same.

27
00:01:21,385 --> 00:01:25,239
Only the mood was different,
and the mood picked the next letters.

28
00:01:25,539 --> 00:01:27,335
It happens all through a review.

29
00:01:27,335 --> 00:01:31,994
After “I would”, a happy review bets on r,
for recommend.

30
00:01:31,994 --> 00:01:35,925
An unhappy one bets on n, for not.

31
00:01:36,025 --> 00:01:37,677
After “Overall I am very”:

32
00:01:37,677 --> 00:01:40,641
h for happy, or d for disappointed.

33
00:01:40,891 --> 00:01:43,819
The mood is set early
and pays off again and again.

34
00:01:43,819 --> 00:01:47,064
So every nudge that helped it
keep track of the mood

35
00:01:47,064 --> 00:01:50,393
made its guesses better,
and those nudges added up.

36
00:01:50,593 --> 00:01:52,781
Nobody asked for a mood detector.

37
00:01:52,781 --> 00:01:55,571
Guessing the next letter well
rewarded one.

38
00:01:56,371 --> 00:01:58,107
So where does it keep the mood?

39
00:01:58,807 --> 00:02:00,464
To guess well, it has to remember

40
00:02:00,464 --> 00:02:01,970
what it has read so far.

41
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:04,320
So the researchers gave it a memory:

42
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:07,783
4,096 neurons, the size they chose.

43
00:02:07,783 --> 00:02:10,511
After every letter, it updates them all.

44
00:02:10,661 --> 00:02:12,049
That memory never grows,

45
00:02:12,049 --> 00:02:14,020
however long the review gets,

46
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:15,155
so it can only keep

47
00:02:15,155 --> 00:02:16,645
what helps the next guess.

48
00:02:16,895 --> 00:02:18,597
After training, the researchers

49
00:02:18,597 --> 00:02:20,182
showed it reviews they knew

50
00:02:20,182 --> 00:02:21,479
were happy or unhappy,

51
00:02:21,479 --> 00:02:24,002
and searched its memory for a neuron

52
00:02:24,002 --> 00:02:25,443
that tracked the difference.

53
00:02:25,443 --> 00:02:28,338
One neuron did, almost by itself.

54
00:02:28,538 --> 00:02:30,647
Put a single bar on that neuron,

55
00:02:30,647 --> 00:02:31,817
like in lesson 1,

56
00:02:31,817 --> 00:02:33,507
and it sorts movie reviews

57
00:02:33,507 --> 00:02:37,186
into good and bad, right 92 times in 100.

58
00:02:37,386 --> 00:02:38,427
So let's call it

59
00:02:38,427 --> 00:02:40,139
the mood detection neuron.

60
00:02:40,139 --> 00:02:42,778
Researchers call it the sentiment neuron.

61
00:02:42,778 --> 00:02:44,989
Nobody wired it, and its training

62
00:02:44,989 --> 00:02:46,986
never used a single label.

63
00:02:46,986 --> 00:02:49,639
It grew on its own, from guessing letters.

64
00:02:50,139 --> 00:02:53,406
“I really love this blender”: up,
as you'd expect.

65
00:02:53,406 --> 00:02:56,682
Your turn:
“I really wanted to love this blender.”

66
00:02:56,682 --> 00:02:58,369
Which way does it move?

67
00:03:00,669 --> 00:03:03,644
Down. It reads the whole phrase,
not just the word.

68
00:03:03,644 --> 00:03:07,717
A bag of words would have counted
“love” as a vote for happy.

69
00:03:08,417 --> 00:03:10,259
Does the network really use it?

70
00:03:10,959 --> 00:03:13,216
Here's the test:
keep every letter the same,

71
00:03:13,216 --> 00:03:15,597
and turn only this one neuron.

72
00:03:15,747 --> 00:03:19,730
After “I would”:
turned to happy, it bets r.

73
00:03:19,730 --> 00:03:22,571
Turned to unhappy, it bets n.

74
00:03:22,821 --> 00:03:25,262
Let it write, and a whole review follows.

75
00:03:25,262 --> 00:03:30,857
Happy: “This blender is sturdy, well made
and works exactly as advertised.”

76
00:03:30,857 --> 00:03:35,469
Unhappy: “This blender was defective
and did not work properly.”

77
00:03:35,669 --> 00:03:40,589
Same network, same first words.
One neuron, turned.

78
00:03:41,489 --> 00:03:43,397
Four lessons, one idea:

79
00:03:43,397 --> 00:03:46,275
a network that has to describe a lot
with a little

80
00:03:46,275 --> 00:03:48,155
is forced to find what matters.

81
00:03:48,305 --> 00:03:50,422
With labels, it found rules.

82
00:03:50,422 --> 00:03:53,303
Filling in blanks, it found word profiles.

83
00:03:53,303 --> 00:03:57,025
Asked only to guess the next letter,
it found the mood.

84
00:03:57,925 --> 00:04:01,965
And this network
had just 4,096 memory neurons.

85
00:04:02,115 --> 00:04:05,923
Scale networks up,
with far more neurons and far more text,

86
00:04:05,923 --> 00:04:08,241
and they have room to invent far more:

87
00:04:08,241 --> 00:04:11,170
concepts of every kind, and even circuits:

88
00:04:11,170 --> 00:04:14,013
groups of neurons that work
like little functions

89
00:04:14,013 --> 00:04:15,886
and chain steps of reasoning.

90
00:04:16,136 --> 00:04:18,363
We'll open those up in another series.
